1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,096
How do you follow one payment through the whole system?

2
00:00:03,146 --> 00:00:06,912
A merchant calls: my customer's payment has been spinning for a minute.

3
00:00:06,962 --> 00:00:12,960
The payment crossed the payment page, ecom, the card zone, an adapter and a card network.

4
00:00:13,010 --> 00:00:17,208
Without one number to follow, someone searches four places and guesses.

5
00:00:17,258 --> 00:00:22,680
In a hospital, your wristband number is on every chart, in every department.

6
00:00:22,730 --> 00:00:31,632
Here, every request gets one trace number that travels with it across both products, and every answer names it, so a caller can quote it to support.

7
00:00:31,682 --> 00:00:37,296
Every log line carries the same number, so a warning leads straight to the request that caused it.

8
00:00:37,346 --> 00:00:46,032
And every line the running system writes is cleaned first: card numbers keep only their first six and last four digits, and secrets are hidden.

9
00:00:46,082 --> 00:00:49,200
Each product sends all of this to its own monitoring.

10
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:55,152
Dashboards show how payments end, and alerts send an email when payments stay unknown too long.

11
00:00:55,202 --> 00:00:57,672
Next: what the merchant gets each day.
